locke-treatise-4.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: locke-treatise-4.logic.r1 has "end_mag_preserve → not_absolute" and "end_mag_preserve → power_laws"; locke-treatise-4.logic.r1.2 and locke-treatise-4.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "in the hands of the magistrate the power can have no other end or measure but to preserve the members of the society in their lives, liberties and possessions" and "the power cannot be an absolute, arbitrary power over the lives and fortunes of the members" are true, which the second allows
locke-treatise-4.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps locke-treatise-4.logic.r1, to: Suppose "in the hands of the magistrate the power can have no other end or measure but to preserve the members of the society in their lives, liberties and possessions" and "the power cannot be an absolute, arbitrary power over the lives and fortunes of the members" are true and "the power is a power to make laws and annex penalties tending to the preservation of the whole, cutting off only those parts so corrupt that they threaten the sound" is false. Is S4, "So that the end and measure of this power, when in every man’s hands in the state of nature, being the preservation of all of his society, that is, all mankind in general, it can have no other end or measure, when in the hands of the magistrate, but to preserve the members of that society in their lives, liberties, and possessions; and so cannot be an absolute, arbitrary power over their lives and fortunes, which are as much as possible to be preserved; but a power to make laws, and annex such penalties to them, as may tend to the preservation of the whole, by cutting off those parts, and those only, which are so corrupt, that they threaten the sound and healthy, without which no severity is lawful.", true then?
locke-treatise-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: locke-treatise-4.logic.r1 has "pp_given_up ∧ end_nat_preservation → end_mag_preserve"; locke-treatise-4.logic.r1.2 has "pp_given_up ∧ end_nat_preservation → end_mag_preserve" and "end_mag_preserve → not_absolute ∧ power_laws"; they differ when only "political power is that power which every man has in the state of nature and has given up to the society and its governors, with trust that it be employed for their good and the preservation of their property", "the end and measure of this power in every man's hands in the state of nature is the preservation of all mankind", "in the hands of the magistrate the power can have no other end or measure but to preserve the members of the society in their lives, liberties and possessions" and "the power cannot be an absolute, arbitrary power over the lives and fortunes of the members" are true, which the first allows; locke-treatise-4.logic.r1.3 has nothing from it
